So the regression introduced `coap_split_proxy_uri` being called on the Proxy-Uri option value. The bug is in `coap_split_uri_sub` when parsing the URI. Let me analyze the IPv6 parsing bug more carefully:

```c
q = p;
if (len && *p == '[') {        /* IPv6 address reference */
    ++p;

    while (len && *q != ']') {
      ++q; --len;
    }

    if (!len || *q != ']' || p == q) {
      res = -3;
      goto error;
    }

    COAP_SET_STR(&uri->host, q - p, p);
    ++q; --len;
```

The bug: `q` is set to `p` (pointing at `[`), then `++p` moves p past `[`. The while loop checks `*q != ']'` and increments `q`. So `q` starts at `[` and moves forward. But `len` is NOT decremented when `++p` happens. So the loop reads `len` bytes starting from `q` (at `[`). The string has `len` bytes total. So it reads exactly `len` bytes - positions 0 to len-1. That's within bounds.

Wait, but actually let me reconsider. The issue is that `q` and `p` diverge. `q` starts at `[` (position 0), `p` starts at position 1. The loop reads `*q` and increments `q` while `len > 0`. So it reads positions 0, 1, 2, ..., len-1. That's `len` bytes, all within the buffer. So no overflow there.

Hmm, but wait. Let me look at the `path:` label logic. After the IPv6 parsing, `q` points to `]` (or wherever the loop stopped). Then:

```c
path:                 /* at this point, p must point to an absolute path */

  if (!len)
    goto end;

  if (*q == '/') {
```

Here `*q` is read. If `len` is 0, we goto end. But if `len > 0` and `q` points past the end of the buffer... Let me think.

Actually, let me reconsider the IPv6 loop. The loop condition is `while (len && *q != ']')`. It reads `*q` and if not `]`, increments `q` and decrements `len`. So the loop stops when either `len == 0` or `*q == ']'`.

Case 1: `*q == ']'` found. Then `q` points at `]`. `len` is still > 0. Then `COAP_SET_STR(&uri->host, q - p, p)` sets host. Then `++q; --len;` moves q past `]`.

Case 2: `len == 0`. Then `q` points one past the last byte. `*q` is read in the check `if (!len || *q != ']' || p == q)` - but `!len` is true so short-circuit, no read. Then `res = -3; goto error;`. So no overflow.

Hmm, so where's the overflow? Let me look at the `path:` section more carefully. After the host parsing (non-IPv6 case):

```c
} else {                        /* IPv4 address or FQDN */
    while (len && *q != ':' && *q != '/' && *q != '?') {
      ++q;
      --len;
    }

    if (p == q) {
      res = -3;
      goto error;
    }

    COAP_SET_STR(&uri->host, q - p, p);
  }

  /* check for Uri-Port */
  if (len && *q == ':') {
    p = ++q;
    --len;

    while (len && isdigit(*q)) {
      ++q;
      --len;
    }
    ...
```

Let me think about the `path:` label. When we reach `path:`, `q` points to either `/`, `?`, or the end. Then:

```c
path:                 /* at this point, p must point to an absolute path */

  if (!len)
    goto end;

  if (*q == '/') {
    p = ++q;
    --len;

    while (len && *q != '?') {
      ++q;
      --len;
    }

    if (p < q) {
      COAP_SET_STR(&uri->path, q - p, p);
      p = q;
    }
  }
```

Here `*q` is read when `len > 0`. If `q` points to the last byte and `len == 1`, then `*q` is valid. If `*q == '/'`, then `p = ++q` moves q past the last byte, `--len` makes len 0. Then the while loop doesn't execute. `p < q` is true, so `COAP_SET_STR(&uri->path, q - p, p)` - path length is 0. That's fine.

Hmm, let me think about the actual overflow differently. The crash is a READ 1 at address 0x612000000a46. Let me look at the actual fuzzer input format. The fuzzer is `pdu_parse_fuzzer` which parses a CoAP PDU. The Proxy-Uri option is what triggers `coap_get_uri_path` -> `coap_split_proxy_uri`.

Let me look at how the PDU is parsed and how options are extracted. Let me check `coap_opt_value` and `coap_opt_length`:

